_refine_special_details
;
Circa one quarter of iodine atoms are replaced by thiophenol groups. 
The phenyl rings are disordered around a three-fold axis roughly 
aligned with the S-C bond axis. The phenyl ring is also bisected by 
three mirror planes (they intersect at the three fold axis), thus 
leading to six-fold disorder for the ortho and meta carbon atoms 
of the phenyl ring. The total occupancy (due to disorder and circa 
quarter occupancy for the thiophenolate as a whole) was freely refined 
but constrained to sum up to unity with that of the iodine atom, 
resulting in occupancies of 0.762(7) for the iodine atom, 0.238(7) 
for the sulfur atom and the ipso C atom (six times 0.0397(11)), and 
0.0397(11) for the other six-fold disordered atoms of the phenyl ring 
(the position of the para C-atom refined to be slightly offset from 
the three-fold axis and is thus also six-fold disordered). Initial 
positions for o-, m- and p-C atoms were resolved in difference density 
Fourier maps, but due to their low occupancy rates positions and ADPs 
needed to be restrained. All C-C bond distances were restrained to a 
target value of 1.39(1) Angstroms. All 1,3 C-C distances were restrained 
to be similar in length (esd 0.01 Angstrom) and the o-C to m-C distances 
across the ring were restrained to be similar (esd 0.01 Angstrom).
The ring C atoms and the S atom were restrained to be coplanar. The ADPs 
of the S and I atom were constrained to be identical. Uij components of 
ADPs of C and S atoms closer to each other than 2.0 Angstrom were 
restrained to be similar. Subject to these conditions the occupancy 
rates refined to the above values. 
Individual phenyl rings are disordered in place around the three fold 
axis and the mirror planes that bisect them, but are also disordered 
with phenyl rings that originate from the next parallel Pb-L layer. 
Phenyl rings originating from parallel layers are related to each other 
by two fold axes perpendicular to the c-axis at a height similar to 
that of the  meta and para C atoms, and by inversion centers at the 
intersection of these 2-fold axes. This necessarily creates overlap
 between phenyl rings from parallel layers and preventing replacement 
of a higher number of iodine atoms by thiophenolate anions. 
Phenyl rings are also in close contact with neighboring phenyl rings 
originating from the same Pb-I layer (close H...H contacts), but the 
six fold rotational disorder allows rings to avoid actual repulsive 
interactions.
While disorder is correlated within every thiophenolate layer there are 
no indicators for correlation across the Pb-I layers. The data do not 
indicate formation of a lower symmetry ordered superstructure. 
;
